{
  "id": "Compound::DB00959 - Disease::DOID:7148",
  "verbalization": "Methylprednisolone is linked to Rheumatoid Arthritis through a shared pathway involving Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist. The evidence suggests that Methylprednisolone, as a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is indirectly involved in the treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis via Hydrocortisone, which is known to treat this condition. This connection is supported by the lowest common ancestor of Methylprednisolone and Hydrocortisone being 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, indicating a common biological pathway. The evidence also shows that Methylprednisolone is associated with Gout and Atopic Dermatitis, both of which are treated by Hydrocortisone, reinforcing the role of Hydrocortisone in the broader context of corticosteroid therapy. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Methylprednisolone to influence Rheumatoid Arthritis through its interaction with Hydrocortisone, suggesting a mechanistic link that is consistent across different disease manifestations.",
